# ResinCompute - line-ending and diff policy.
#
# EOL POLICY, and it is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
#
# Sibling-C runs core.autocrlf=true on its Windows fleet and paid for it
# twice: 21 tracked .py files ended up with doubled CR line endings (the
# 2026-05-19 rc_facts.py incident), and a byte-compared generated sentinel came
# back CRLF on every fresh clone. Forcing eol=lf pins the bytes in the repo AND
# in the working tree, which overrides autocrlf so neither can recur.
#
# The .githooks/ shims are the sharper case. They are `#!/bin/sh` scripts. A
# CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named
# "/bin/sh\r", which does not exist - so the hook fails with an unreadable
# "bad interpreter" error, or on some setups is skipped, and the whole
# commit-time gate is silently absent. A gate that is absent without saying so
# is the failure mode this scaffold exists to prevent.

# Sensible default: git decides what is text, and normalises it to LF in the
# repo.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the surfaces where a wrong ending is a real failure, not a
# diff nuisance.
*.py       text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.ini      text eol=lf
*.toml     text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers, if any are ever added, must keep CRLF - cmd.exe and
# PowerShell 5.1 both mis-parse an LF-only script in places.
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.ps1      text eol=crlf

# Binary - never diffed, never EOL-converted.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.ico      binary
*.gz       binary
*.zip      binary
*.db       binary
